Changes to Mesa 20.3.5 for RISC OS, applied in this order by
build/build-mesa.sh (which records them in .riscos-patches-applied in the
Mesa tree):

mesa-20.3.5-riscos.patch
    The port itself (OS detection, no ELF TLS, UnixLib gaps, a static
    libOSMesa, the OSMesa ES 1.1/2.0 profiles).

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-speed.patch
    Speed-ups for the software renderer. Rendering is unchanged (checked
    image by image against the unpatched library, on the host and on ARM),
    except where noted:
    - swrast/s_depth.c: 24-bit depth buffers (Z24_UNORM_X8_UINT, what EGL,
      SDL and GLUT ask for) are tested in place, like 16- and 32-bit ones,
      instead of copying each span to a malloc'd buffer and back.
    - osmesa/osmesa.c: the fast depth-tested triangles work with 24-bit
      depth buffers and GL_LEQUAL as well as 16-bit GL_LESS. Before this
      they were only used with 16-bit depth.
    - swrast/s_texfetch_tmp.h: texels of the common 8-bit formats (RGBA,
      BGRA, RGB, L, A, LA, I, R, RG) are read directly instead of through
      a call per texel into the general unpack code.
    - swrast/s_triangle.c: Mesa's integer fast path for textured triangles
      (one texture, no mipmaps, GL_REPEAT, power-of-two size) now also
      takes GL_RGBA/GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE textures (R8G8B8A8), which it
      previously skipped. It rounds slightly differently from the general
      path (at most 2/255 per channel). RGBA textures stay
      perspective-correct under GL_FASTEST.
    - program/prog_execute.c: the GLSL/ARB program interpreter's operand
      fetch and store are always inlined.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-glsl-decode.patch
    program/prog_execute.c: GLSL and ARB programs (vertex and fragment)
    are decoded once into a compact form, so the interpreter no longer
    decodes every operand (register file, range checks, swizzle, negate)
    again for each fragment and vertex. The arithmetic is unchanged, so
    the results are identical. The decoded copy is kept in the
    interpreter's machine state and checked against the program once per
    span and per vertex batch (swrast/s_fragprog.c, tnl/t_vb_program.c),
    so it's rebuilt whenever a program is changed or replaced; operands
    with relative addressing use the original code.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-glsl-batch.patch
    program/prog_execute.c, swrast/s_fragprog.c: fragment programs run
    for up to 32 fragments at a time. Each instruction is dispatched once
    and applied to every active fragment, with branch-free loops for the
    common case (no negation, relative addressing or saturation). Control
    flow is followed with per-fragment masks: IF/ELSE/ENDIF, loops with
    BRK and CONT, KIL (discard) and RET from main; each fragment has its
    own address register for relative addressing. Programs with
    subroutines (CAL) run one fragment at a time as before, and so does a
    batch that hits the runaway-loop limit, so that limit applies to each
    fragment exactly as before. The per-fragment arithmetic is generated
    from the interpreter's own code by tools/gen-glsl-batch.py (the part
    between the BEGIN/END GENERATED comments in prog_execute.c: edit the
    interpreter's cases, run the tool, never edit that part by hand), so
    results are identical, except
    where a program reads a register it hasn't written (undefined in
    GLSL), which sees that fragment's own earlier value rather than the
    previous fragment's.

mesa-20.3.5-gcc13-vectorizer.patch
    GCC 13 at -O3 (on x86-64, and on ARM with NEON) miscompiles
    interpolate_int_colors() in swrast/s_span.c: some pixels of smooth
    shaded spans come out R,G,R,G. This turns off vectorisation for that
    one function. GCCSDK GCC 10 isn't affected (the RISC OS build uses VFP
    without NEON); it's here for host builds such as the test harness.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-startup.patch
    mapi/glapi/glapi_getproc.c: GL functions are found by name with a
    binary search in an index sorted by name (built once, on first use),
    instead of comparing the name with each of the 2339 built-in functions
    in turn. Creating the first context looks up about 2500 names
    (_mesa_init_remap_table), which cost ~87 million instructions, about
    0.1 s on a Raspberry Pi 4, before a program drew anything; now ~3
    million (creating a context: 93.5 -> 10.3 million, measured on the
    host).
    GetProcAddress lookups (eglGetProcAddress, SDL_GL_GetProcAddress) use
    the same search. Where a name occurs twice the first entry in table
    order is returned, as before.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-size-limit.patch
    The largest surface, viewport, renderbuffer and texture is 4096 x 4096
    (Mesa: 16384). swrast sizes each context's span buffers by the largest
    width, and RISC OS commits all of it in the application slot. A
    context allocates 9.6 MB instead of 22.5 MB (measured on the host). 4096 covers every Pi screen mode up to 4K
    and is riscos-mesa's EGL pbuffer limit.
    - swrast/swrast.h: SWRAST_MAX_WIDTH/HEIGHT 4096, and
      program/prog_execute.h PROG_MAX_WIDTH to match (now a compile-time
      check in swrast/s_context.c).
    - osmesa/osmesa.c: set_size_limits() reports swrast's limits to GL
      (GL_MAX_VIEWPORT_DIMS, GL_MAX_RENDERBUFFER_SIZE,
      GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE and the rectangle, 3D and cube map limits).
      Before, GL offered 16384 although swrast couldn't handle it.
    - swrast: four debug asserts (s_masking.c, s_logic.c, s_lines.c,
      s_copypix.c) required a span to be shorter than the maximum; a span
      exactly as wide as the buffers is valid, and is reachable now that
      the maximum is a real screen width.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-span-speed.patch
    Per-span speed-ups found by profiling glbench's scenes. The output is
    identical (the image checks in tests/host-harness/mesa fail if either
    of the first two paths is broken, so they are covered):
    - swrast/s_triangle.c (perspective-correct textured triangles): 1/q
      was a double divide for every pixel, 20-30 cycles each on a
      Cortex-A72. When q doesn't change along the span (2D, orthographic
      and screen-aligned drawing: HUDs, sprites, text) it's the same for
      every pixel, so it's worked out once per span (PERSP_INV_Q).
    - swrast/s_span.c (interpolate_int_colors): a smooth-shaded span whose
      colour steps are all zero (a primitive drawn in one colour) is
      filled with that colour instead of stepping four interpolants per
      pixel. glbench's blend scene: 12.6% fewer instructions.
    - main/image.c (_mesa_convert_colors): the temporary buffer is only
      allocated for an in-place conversion. swrast converts every span of
      fragment program output (float to ubyte, never in place), so each
      span did a malloc and free for nothing.

mesa-20.3.5-riscos-direct-rows.patch
    swrast/s_span.c: when the colour buffer holds RGBA8 pixels in swrast's
    own byte order (R, G, B, A: OSMesa's OSMESA_RGBA, the usual RISC OS
    screen layout), rows are used as they are:
    - _swrast_get_dest_rgba (blending, logic ops, colour masking) returns
      a pointer to the row in the buffer instead of unpacking it into a
      temporary array, if the span is a whole row inside the buffer and
      the address is 4-byte aligned (masking and logic ops read it as
      32-bit words; a program's own OSMesa buffer need not be aligned).
      Its callers only read through the pointer.
    - _swrast_put_row copies GL_UNSIGNED_BYTE rows with memcpy instead of
      packing them pixel by pixel (put_ubyte_run).
    Output identical (tests/host-harness/mesa/render-rows.c draws each
    case aligned and misaligned). glbench's blend scene: 9.3% fewer
    instructions.

They are under the licence of the files they change: MIT (Mesa), except
the change to include/c11/threads_posix.h, which is under the Boost
Software License 1.0. See LICENCES.txt.

To change them, work on a git branch rather than on the patch files:

    tools/mesa-branch.sh make ~/mesa-riscos    # pristine Mesa + one commit per patch
    ...edit, commit (git commit --amend / rebase -i onto the patch's commit)...
    tools/mesa-branch.sh export ~/mesa-riscos  # writes the patch files back

Each commit's message ends with "Patch: <name>", which says which file it
is exported to. A new patch is a new commit with a new "Patch:" line; add
its name to PATCHES in build/build-mesa.sh and describe it here. The patch
files are plain git diffs (patch -p1).

After changing any of these patches, apply them to a host Mesa tree,
rebuild it and run tests/run-all.sh (rendering and performance checks in
tests/host-harness/mesa).
